Lapilli statically links the Rust crates listed here into every binary it publishes: the
`lapilli-controller` and `lapilli` binaries in the container image, and the `lapilli` CLI in each
release tarball. Their licences require the licence text and the copyright notices to be
distributed with those binaries, so this file travels with them —
`/usr/local/share/doc/lapilli/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md` in the image, and the top level of each
tarball.

Lapilli's own licence is [Apache-2.0](LICENSE). Notices from dependencies that ship a `NOTICE`
file of their own are reproduced in [`NOTICE`](NOTICE), not here.

Where a crate offers a choice of licences, the one reproduced below is the one taken: Apache-2.0
wherever it is offered, so that most of the graph is covered by the same licence as Lapilli
itself. The full expression a crate declares is in its own `Cargo.toml`. The same licence appears
more than once where crates ship textually different copies of it — a different copyright line, a
filled-in appendix — and each copy is reproduced as its own copyright holders wrote it. A crate
whose terms are conjunctive (`ring`'s `Apache-2.0 AND ISC`, `matchit`'s `MIT AND BSD-3-Clause`)
appears under each of them, because there is no choice to make: both apply. So the counts below
add up to more than the number of crates.

Lapilli's own workspace crates (`lapilli-bundle`, `lapilli-cli`, `lapilli-controller`,
`lapilli-kms`, `lapilli-net`) are in the listing too, under Apache-2.0. They are not third party,
but they *are* linked in, and leaving them out would make this file disagree with the dependency
graph the release gate checks it against.

Covered targets and the choice of features are in [`about.toml`](about.toml): the union of the
five target triples Lapilli publishes binaries for, with all features on, so a
platform-conditional or feature-gated dependency is not dropped from this listing.

